      "title": "Blackmail, ‘mafia boys’ and rants from a Beirut bedroom – after 10 weeks of theatre what might Icac deliver?",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/30/icac-nsw-liberals-what-did-inquiry-really-achieve-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>You could be forgiven for thinking the inquiry has been a bit of a damp squib. But high-profile resignations are not the only measure of Icac’s success</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Many people watching the Independent Commission Against Corruption hearings in Sydney over the past two months have come to admire the forensic questioning of the counsel assisting the inquiry, Peggy Dwyer SC.</p><p>As she <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/jul/27/icac-operation-rosny-nsw-liberals-corruption-investigation-ntwnfb\">set out the complicated allegations</a> in detail on the opening day in July, Dwyer emphasised the central role of electoral donations laws, observing: “Money in politics, as in other spheres of life, has the potential to corrupt.”</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/30/icac-nsw-liberals-what-did-inquiry-really-achieve-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-29T15:00:54"
    },
    {
      "title": "Senior Greens figures say new leadership must stop Labor approving fossil fuel projects ‘by whatever means’",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/30/senior-greens-figures-say-new-leadership-must-stop-labor-approving-fossil-fuel-projects-by-whatever-means",
      "description": "<p>No excuses for next leader of party, Adam Bandt says, while Bob Brown warns they must also be able to take on Pauline Hanson and One Nation</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>The former Greens leader Adam Bandt says the party’s new leadership team, which could be chosen as early as today, will face mounting pressure from grassroots members to block the Labor government from approving any new coal and gas projects “by whatever means”.</p><p>He told Guardian Australia there would be no excuses for the new leader, likely to be elected at a two-day retreat in Sydney which begins today, to allow the Albanese government to approve any new fossil fuel development.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/30/senior-greens-figures-say-new-leadership-must-stop-labor-approving-fossil-fuel-projects-by-whatever-means\">Continue reading...</a>",
